SCALING / NORMALIZATION

feature scaling using a technique called Standardization (Z-score normalization)
This line standardizes the Age and Salary columns by transforming their values to have:
Mean = 0
Standard deviation = 1

Why is this important?
Some ML models are scale-sensitive:

Algorithms like SVM, KNN, neural networks, logistic regression can behave badly if features have very different scales (e.g., age in years vs. salary in dollars).

Prevents dominance:
Features like "Salary" might dominate smaller values like "Age", skewing the model’s learning.
Improves convergence:
Gradient descent (used in many models) converges faster when features are normalized.

StandardScaler() computes mean and std from the training data only and applies the same transformation to test data.

If you use a pipeline, scaling should happen after splitting into train/test, to prevent data leakage.

max-min scaling 
(x - xmin)/(xmax - xmin)
Scales values to a fixed range, typically [0, 1]

Robust scaling
Max ab scaling Scales data by the maximum absolute value, so all values are in [-1, 1]


