Removing or handling outliers is a key step in data cleaning — especially if you're using models that are sensitive to extreme values, like linear regression or SVMs.

An outlier is a data point that lies far outside the normal range of your dataset — it can be due to:
Errors in data entry, Measurement noise, Genuinely rare but valid cases

 Techniques to Detect & Remove Outliers are:

-Z-Score Method (Standard Deviation Rule)
Assumes data is normally distributed.
Outliers are points that are far from the mean:
z = (x-u)/sigma

- IQR Method (Interquartile Range)

- Percentile Capping / Winsorization
Replace extreme values with a capped value.
Useful for reducing effect without dropping data.

-. Isolation Forest (Machine Learning-Based)
Unsupervised algorithm that isolates outliers by building trees.


Choose a method based on:
| Data Type           | Recommended Method       |
| ------------------- | ------------------------ |
| Normal distribution | Z-score, IQR             |
| Non-normal          | IQR, Percentile capping  |
| Multivariate        | Isolation Forest, DBSCAN |
| Small dataset       | Visual + IQR             |

