ENCODING CATEGORICAL VARIABLES 

Encoding categorical variables is essential for machine learning models, which typically cannot work with raw text or labels. The right encoding method depends on the nature of your categorical data — whether it's nominal (no order) or ordinal (ordered).

| Categorical Input             | Machine Learning Needs               |
| ----------------------------- | ------------------------------------ |
| `'Red'`, `'Blue'`             | Numeric values (e.g., `0`, `1`, ...) |
| `'Low'`, `'Medium'`, `'High'` | Ordered numeric encoding             |
| `'Yes'`, `'No'`               | Binary encoding                      |

-Label encoding
Each category is assigned a unique integer.

--One-Hot Encoding
Creates binary columns for each category.
| Color\_Red | Color\_Blue | Color\_Green |
| ---------- | ----------- | ------------ |
| 1          | 0           | 0            |
| 0          | 1           | 0            |
| 0          | 0           | 1            |

-Ordinal Encoding: Manually map categories to ordered numbers.
-Binary Encoding: Encodes categories into binary form and splits them into columns.
-Frequency Encoding: Replace category with its frequency in the dataset.
- Target / Mean Encoding: Replace category with the mean target value for that category.

Choosing the right encoding 
| Feature Type        | Recommended Encoding      |
| ------------------- | ------------------------- |
| Nominal (unordered) | One-Hot, Frequency        |
| Ordinal (ordered)   | Label, Ordinal            |
| High cardinality    | Binary, Frequency, Target |
| Tree-based models   | Any (Label works well)    |
| Linear models       | One-Hot, Target Encoding  |

