FEATURE ENGINEERING

Feature engineering is one of the most powerful tools in a data scientist’s toolkit. It involves creating, transforming, or selecting features to help a machine learning model learn better patterns from the data.

Here’s a comprehensive breakdown of techniques and methods used in feature engineering:

- Feature Creation: Generate new variables from existing ones.
Date decomposition: From timestamp → year, month, day, weekday
Text length: text_length = len(text)
Interaction terms: new_feature = feature1 * feature2
Binning: Convert a continuous value into categories (e.g., Age → Young, Adult, Old)

-Feature Transformation: Convert features into forms that better suit the model.
Log transformation (for skewed data)
Standardization / Normalization
Box-Cox / Yeo-Johnson transformation
Encoding categorical features (Label, One-Hot, Ordinal)

-Handling missing values
Imputation (mean, median, mode, interpolation)
Indicator variables: Add a binary column indicating if a value was missing


-Feature Selection
Choose only the most relevant features for training.
Methods:
Univariate statistics (e.g., ANOVA, chi-squared test)
Model-based (e.g., feature importance from Random Forests, Lasso)
Recursive Feature Elimination (RFE)
Correlation analysis to remove multicollinearity

-Dimensionality reduction 
Dimensionality Reduction
Reduce the number of features while preserving the information.
Techniques:
Principal Component Analysis (PCA)
t-SNE / UMAP (for visualization)
Autoencoders (deep learning)

-Domain-Specific Feature Design
Based on knowledge of the problem domain:
In finance: Moving averages, returns, volatility
In NLP: Word count, TF-IDF, sentiment scores
In images: Edge detection, histograms, pixel intensities

-Target Encoding / Mean Encoding: Replace a category with the mean of the target variable for that category.

- Polynomial Features: Add interaction or higher-degree terms to capture non-linear patterns.

-Discretization / Quantization: Convert continuous variables into discrete bins:


